messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nTo determine whether the statement "Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given information, let\'s analyze the logical relationships step by step.\n\n1.**Mary works in student jobs on campus**: This means she earns money to help pay for her college tuition.\n2.**Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition**: This is consistent with the first point.\n3.**If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition**: This is a general rule that applies to Mary.\n4.**If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus**: This implies that Mary might order takeout frequently.\n5.**People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes**: This means Mary either orders takeout frequently or enjoys the dining hall meals and recipes.\n6.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters**: This suggests that Mary might not be a picky eater if she enjoys the dining hall meals.\n7.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls**: This indicates that Mary might spend a lot of time eating and catching up with friends if she enjoys the dining hall meals.\n\nNow, let\'s evaluate the statement:\n- **Mary is both a picky eater and, if she isn\'t, then she rarely spends her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls**.\n\nFrom the analysis:\n- If Mary dislikes the dining hall meals and recipes, she is a picky eater.\n- If Mary dislikes the dining hall meals and recipes, she rarely spends her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n\nTherefore, the statement is false because it contradicts the logical implications derived from the given information.\n\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
